      "providerContinuationUnsettled": false,
      "rawResponse": "ASSIST\nFor zero-weight edges, keep BFS but the distance to every vertex reachable from the source is zero. Relax an edge only if it improves the neighbor’s recorded distance; update its parent when it does, and enqueue it if needed. With all weights zero, a FIFO queue still works, and each vertex needs processing at most once after its distance becomes zero, so the time is O(V + E)—about O(60,000) for 10,000 vertices and 50,000 edges. The earlier mark-on-enqueue rule remains safe here if you initialize distances to infinity and set them to zero on first discovery.",
